//! stdlib/testmod/module_name_that_is_definitely_longer_than_sixty_four_bytes_for_testing.sio
//! Witness fixture for PR #2515 review round 2, finding C (comment_id
//! 4089110718): the module-name registries this PR added
//! (LOWER_USE_MODULE_HASH / LOWER_IMPL_METHOD_HASH in self-hosted/ir/lower.sio,
//! and their checker/specializer-side siblings) originally capped each
//! stored segment at 64 bytes and silently refused to register (or match
//! against) anything longer -- not an error, just a quiet no-op -- so a
//! `use` of a module whose name (this file's own base name, which is what
//! `use testmod::<name>;` resolves to) is longer than that cap still
//! parsed and typechecked, but its qualified calls fell back to the
//! mangled body-less stub path (SIGILL), the exact failure class these
//! registries exist to prevent. This file's own name is 71 bytes, deliberately
//! chosen to exceed the old 64-byte cap while staying under the fixed cap
//! (384 bytes, matching `Name.buf: [i8; 384]`).

pub fn long_module_name_add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 with Mut, Panic {
    a + b + 1000
}
